STS-Oct-M23-Hamlett	1-15	16-425	> From today 's Chronicle of Higher Education , Section 2 , front page :	" The Perils of Democratizing Science , " by Normal Levitt & Paul R. Gross " The old respect [ for science ] ... is being supplanted by hostile criticism , which ranges from the tendentious to ludicrous ... Unlike the manifestations of hatred of science in the past , which arose typically from religious orthodoxy or Romantic conservatism , contemporary science bashing is a sport of the academic left . Radical critics insist that science is no more than a social artifact , that its ideas are " constructed " purely on the basis of social pressures and prejudices ... ... Any recognition that science pursued according to these viewpoints [ i.e. , according to the " interests of the dispossessed " ] might , in fact , simply be warped by new , though different , political biases is suppressed by scholars steeped in the " sociology of scientific knowledge . " This perspective is firmly grounded in relativism , the central point of which is that any community 's " : way of knowing " is as good as any other 's . Why not , then , " construct " a science that flatters _your_ community 's hopes ? " " The proposition that science is merely socially determined convention , that it is ridden with the biases of a hermetic scientific community , that scientists pursue not truth but profitable consensus -- such opinions are not only held by a number of ambitious academics ; they also appear to be gaining ground , albeit in toned - down form , among a vastly larger audience of journalists , politicians , students , and general readers . Absorbing aspects of these ideological judgments leads otherwise intelligent people to take seriously the idea that the way science " works " is just a matter of negotiation and persuasion . " " Many intellectuals , new to debates over scientific issues , are convinced that the apparent difficulty of science lies in its jargon , not in the subtlety of its content , and that the ability to contest scientific positions requires only learning the sociology and politics of science . Thus they ignore the wrestling with concepts that is the heart of science education . Even worse , they overlook the methodological savvy needed to understand complex phenomena -- the need to have some grasp of the principles of probability and statistics , for example . "	> From today's Chronicle of Higher Education , Section 2 , front page :	" The Perils of Democratizing Science , " by Normal Levitt & Paul R. Gross " The old respect [ for science ] ... is being supplanted by hostile criticism , which ranges from the tendentious to ludicrous ... Unlike the manifestations of hatred of science in the past , which arose typically from religious orthodoxy or Romantic conservatism , contemporary science bashing is a sport of the academic left . Radical critics insist that science is no more than a social artifact , that its ideas are " constructed " purely on the basis of social pressures and prejudices ... ... Any recognition that science pursued according to these viewpoints [ i.e. , according to the " interests of the dispossessed " ] might , in fact , simply be warped by new , though different , political biases is suppressed by scholars steeped in the " sociology of scientific knowledge . " This perspective is firmly grounded in relativism , the central point of which is that any community's " : way of knowing " is as good as any other's . Why not , then , " construct " a science that flatters _your_ community's hopes ? " " The proposition that science is merely socially determined convention , that it is ridden with the biases of a hermetic scientific community , that scientists pursue not truth but profitable consensus -- such opinions are not only held by a number of ambitious academics ; they also appear to be gaining ground , albeit in toned - down form , among a vastly larger audience of journalists , politicians , students , and general readers . Absorbing aspects of these ideological judgments leads otherwise intelligent people to take seriously the idea that the way science " works " is just a matter of negotiation and persuasion . " " Many intellectuals , new to debates over scientific issues , are convinced that the apparent difficulty of science lies in its jargon , not in the subtlety of its content , and that the ability to contest scientific positions requires only learning the sociology and politics of science . Thus they ignore the wrestling with concepts that is the heart of science education . Even worse , they overlook the methodological savvy needed to understand complex phenomena -- the need to have some grasp of the principles of probability and statistics , for example . "	1-54	1-54	> From today 's Chronicle of Higher Education , Section 2 , front page : " The Perils of Democratizing Science , " by Normal Levitt & Paul R. Gross " The old respect [ for science ] ... is being supplanted by hostile criticism , which ranges from the tendentious to ludicrous ...	> From today 's Chronicle of Higher Education , Section 2 , front page : " The Perils of Democratizing Science , " by Normal Levitt & Paul R. Gross " The old respect [ for science ] ... is being supplanted by hostile criticism , which ranges from the tendentious to ludicrous ...	1>2	unknown	preparation	organization
