STS-Oct-M36-Frost	1-4	5-166	Paul Edwards writes :	> If LIEBERMAN or anyone else genuinely believes that STS convincingly > demonstrates that external reality does n't exist , I would like to be > notified of just where and how this has been done . On my reading , most STS > literature asserts , and often demonstrates , some form of interpretive > flexibility in scientific claims or technological design . But there is a > vast difference between flexibility , an excellent metaphor whose > connotations should be taken seriously , and infinite malleability - which > does n't even work as a metaphor ( from what experiential background could it > be drawn ? ) , much less as a coherent literal description of anything at all . > The most common philosophical mistake in STS is to leap from the > possibility of _multiple_ interpretations or constructions to the > possibility of an _infinite_ number . When something flexes too far , it > breaks .	Paul Edwards writes :	> If LIEBERMAN or anyone else genuinely believes that STS convincingly > demonstrates that external reality doesn't exist , I would like to be > notified of just where and how this has been done . On my reading , most STS > literature asserts , and often demonstrates , some form of interpretive > flexibility in scientific claims or technological design . But there is a > vast difference between flexibility , an excellent metaphor whose > connotations should be taken seriously , and infinite malleability - which > doesn't even work as a metaphor ( from what experiential background could it > be drawn ? ) , much less as a coherent literal description of anything at all . > The most common philosophical mistake in STS is to leap from the > possibility of _multiple_ interpretations or constructions to the > possibility of an _infinite_ number . When something flexes too far , it > breaks .	1-41	1-41	Paul Edwards writes : > If LIEBERMAN or anyone else genuinely believes that STS convincingly > demonstrates that exter