STS-Oct-M80-Bailar	1-6	7-142	Fuller says , in part :	" It 's not that hard to open up space for scientists to voice their opinions in science studies narratives . In fact , this is routinely done . The tough question -- which EDWARDS has n't really addressed -- is how much authority you grant those opinions in directing the course of your own narrative . Usually , this question is answered by simply not addressing your narrative to scientists ( or hoping they do n't read you ! ) . In that case , scientists ' words are quickly converted to symptoms ( or ' signs ' , if one is a bit more neutral ) . But scientists are now beginning to take our stuff seriously , and so this particular form of evasion is not going to work much longer . "	Fuller says , in part :	" It's not that hard to open up space for scientists to voice their opinions in science studies narratives . In fact , this is routinely done . The tough question -- which EDWARDS hasn't really addressed -- is how much authority you grant those opinions in directing the course of your own narrative . Usually , this question is answered by simply not addressing your narrative to scientists ( or hoping they don't read you ! ) . In that case , scientists ' words are quickly converted to symptoms ( or ' signs ' , if one is a bit more neutral ) . But scientists are now beginning to take our stuff seriously , and so this particular form of evasion is not going to work much longer . "	1-27	1-27	Fuller says , in part : " It 's not that hard to open up space for scientists to voice their opinions in science studies narratives .	Fuller says , in part : " It 's not that hard to open up space for scientists to voice their opinions in science studies narratives .	1>2	unknown	attribution	attribution
